<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rupert of Germany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1352">1352</a>–<a href="/years/1410">1410</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Rupert of the Palatinate, Ruprecht III · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rupert of the Palatinate was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a> and King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> from 1400, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> deposed Wenceslaus, until his death in 1410. His authority was limited, and an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> failed. His sons divided the Palatinate lands, and the marriage of his son Stephen to Anna of Veldenz founded the house of Palatinate-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zweibrucken">Zweibrücken</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rupert-of-germany.jpg" alt="Rupert of Germany" width="320" height="282" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diözese Speyer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rupprecht_III._von_der_Pfalz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Elector <b>Rupert III</b> was himself elected King of the Romans in 1400, after the deposition of Wenceslaus, and reigned until 1410.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Frederick III died in 1444 without sons; his daughter Anna had married Stephen, son of <b>King Rupert</b> of the Palatinate.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-veldenz">County of Veldenz</a></li><li><blockquote>George died in 1503 without sons, leaving his lands to his son-in-law <b>Rupert of the Palatinate</b> in breach of Wittelsbach house law.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-landshut">Duchy of Bavaria-Landshut</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the mayor Heinrich Toppler, who died in prison in 1408 after the city fell foul of <b>King Rupert</b>, the city built up one of the largest rural territories of any imperial city.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-rothenburg">Free Imperial City of Rothenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>King Rupert</b>, Elector Palatine, died in 1410, his lands were divided among his four sons.</blockquote><a href="/state/palatinate-mosbach">Palatinate-Mosbach</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cologne arbitration of 1505 that ended the War of the Landshut Succession created a new principality from parts of the Duchy of Bavaria-Landshut for Otto Henry and Philip, the young sons of <b>Rupert of the Palatinate</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a></li></ul></section>
